// Completion batch 4: original lesson-grounded provisional worked analyses. Append only, preserve existing guides.
(function(){'use strict';const courses=window.LAW_LEVEL4||[];const lessons=courses.flatMap(c=>c.lessons||[]);const additions=[{"id":"L4-4-13","threshold":"The core legal question in Appellate drafting: harmless error, standards of review and remedy is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Separate error, preservation and prejudice; distinguish cumulative testimony from indispensable excluded expert proof.","analysis":"The fictional record states: A federal trial judge excluded a key expert declaration but admitted related lay testimony. A jury rejected the plaintiff's claim; the plaintiff appeals and seeks a new trial. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under 28 U.S.C. §2111; Fed. R. App. P.; relevant circuit precedent. Separate error, preservation and prejudice; distinguish cumulative testimony from indispensable excluded expert proof. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Write an appellate jurisdiction statement, 1,200-word merits section with citations placeholders, 600-word opposition, harmless-error table and proposed remand language. The underlying lesson further requires: Identify a final decision or other valid jurisdictional basis, notice of appeal timing, record citations and the standards governing the underlying ruling and harmless error. Distinguish preservation, error, prejudice and the remedy requested. Build an issue-by-issue standard-of-review chart rather than treating every evidentiary ruling as reviewed de novo. Evaluate whether improperly excluded evidence would have materially affected the verdict given testimony the jury heard. Identify procedural facts needed to establish a meaningful offer of proof and demonstrate reversible error. Draft a fact statement that accurately acknowledges unfavorable evidence. Consider narrow remand versus new trial and why appellate courts often do not resolve factual dis","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Appellant argues expert evidence supplied otherwise absent proof; appellee says cumulative testimony and lack of preservation make any error harmless. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Unique excluded causation evidence versus lay proof already admitted on the same issue. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of 28 U.S.C. §2111; Fed. R. App. P.; relevant circuit precedent. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Write an appellate jurisdiction statement, 1,200-word merits section with citations placeholders, 600-word opposition, harmless-error table and proposed remand language. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-4-14","threshold":"The core legal question in Supreme Court litigation lab: emergency application and narrow holdings is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Differentiate emergency administrative stay, stay pending appeal and merits certiorari; verify jurisdiction and record.","analysis":"The fictional record states: A lower court issues an injunction with nationwide consequences involving government data collection. Litigants ask the Supreme Court for emergency intervention while ordinary appellate proceedings continue. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Supreme Court Rules, jurisdictional statutes, controlling constitutional precedent. Differentiate emergency administrative stay, stay pending appeal and merits certiorari; verify jurisdiction and record. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Draft a 12-page-equivalent brief outline, a two-page emergency fact appendix plan, an opposition argument map and a neutral bench memo with at least three possible narrow dispositions. The underlying lesson further requires: Identify the nature of relief requested, jurisdiction and the proper procedural vehicle before considering equities. Research current Supreme Court Rules and relevant emergency-application doctrine rather than assuming any case qualifies for an emergency order. Distinguish stay of lower-court relief from certiorari, merits relief and interim administrative orders. Identify what is known from the record, what issue was actually decided below and why a narrowly tailored interim order may be preferable. Prepare a neutral assessment of public impact, harm to opposing parties, likelihood of legal success and legal uncertainty. Acknowledge that emergency decisions and subsequent merits opinions have different precedential implications.\n\nWORK METHOD: (1) id","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Applicant says immediate nationwide harm will occur; respondents dispute jurisdiction, record adequacy and asserted irreparable injury. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Narrow temporary relief protecting review versus broad merits resolution on disputed facts. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Supreme Court Rules, jurisdictional statutes, controlling constitutional precedent. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Draft a 12-page-equivalent brief outline, a two-page emergency fact appendix plan, an opposition argument map and a neutral bench memo with at least three possible narrow dispositions. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-5-7","threshold":"The core legal question in Compliance monitoring, audits and remediation evidence is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Match every corrective measure to regulator order, tests, owner, due date and objective evidence.","analysis":"The fictional record states: A transportation company says it completed a promised corrective-action program, but internal auditors found missing testing logs and no designated owner for several tasks. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Applicable regulatory order and industry rules; contractually or legally required recordkeeping and audit standards. Match every corrective measure to regulator order, tests, owner, due date and objective evidence. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Create a 30/60/90-day audit plan, evidence-based compliance dashboard specification, exception register and two-sided agency status memo. Include a verified-source table, factual uncertainties, a second legally plausible outcome and the limits of your chosen remedy. Submit this draft for qualified human review. The underlying lesson further requires: A credible remediation program is a set of verifiable controls rather than a presentation claiming success. Convert each binding order term into a measurable requirement, owner, due date, proof of completion and escalation path. Confirm which records are legally required to exist and how long they must be preserved. When logs are missing, distinguish a failure to perform a test from a failure to document a completed test; both may have consequences, but they are not the same factual proposition. Construct a sampling plan that can reveal systematic gaps without pretending a convenience sample represents all equipment. Prepare a transparent exception register with discovered date, root-cause hypothesis, corrective action, responsible person and retest ","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Regulator seeks verified completion; operator emphasizes substantial effort and disputes whether every gap violates the order. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Independent complete testing logs versus unsourced claims of compliance. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Applicable regulatory order and industry rules; contractually or legally required recordkeeping and audit standards. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Create a 30/60/90-day audit plan, evidence-based compliance dashboard specification, exception register and two-sided agency status memo. Include a verified-source table, factual uncertainties, a second legally plausible outcome and the limits of your chosen remedy. Submit this draft for qualified human review. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-5-8","threshold":"The core legal question in Regulatory inspections, warrants and consent is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Check agency-specific inspection statute, consent, warrant requirement and emergency or regulated-industry exceptions.","analysis":"The fictional record states: A safety inspector asks a plant manager to enter locked production areas, and the manager is unsure whether refusal would trigger sanctions. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Fourth Amendment administrative search doctrine; relevant agency inspection statute and regulations. Check agency-specific inspection statute, consent, warrant requirement and emergency or regulated-industry exceptions. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Prepare an inspection authorization matrix, manager response protocol, evidence-handling plan and two-sided legal memo. Include verified primary citations, adverse arguments, procedural assumptions, a two-outcome sensitivity analysis, and an evidence inventory for examiner review. The underlying lesson further requires: Identify the type of regulated industry, inspection authority, actual statutory conditions and whether a warrant, lawful exception or valid consent is available. Administrative inspections differ from ordinary criminal searches, and special rules may govern closely regulated industries; do not assume either limitless access or an absolute warrant requirement. Distinguish a routine inspection from an emergency and identify who has authority to consent on behalf of the facility. Record the inspection scope, records requested, permitted observation and personnel interviews without coaching employees to obstruct or destroy material. Explain possible administrative and judicial review mechanisms, sanctions for unlawful refusal where applicable and preserv","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Agency asserts statutory authority and safety urgency; operator questions scope and the availability of neutral review. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Voluntary informed consent or lawful warrant versus ambiguous manager authorization. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Fourth Amendment administrative search doctrine; relevant agency inspection statute and regulations. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Prepare an inspection authorization matrix, manager response protocol, evidence-handling plan and two-sided legal memo. Include verified primary citations, adverse arguments, procedural assumptions, a two-outcome sensitivity analysis, and an evidence inventory for examiner review. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-5-9","threshold":"The core legal question in Internal controls, director oversight and remediation reporting is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Review oversight red flags, actual board response, committee reporting and corporate law standards.","analysis":"The fictional record states: A board committee learns that compliance alerts were dismissed for months and that a regulator is considering a corporate reporting agreement. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Corporate oversight duties under governing entity law; agency enforcement terms; relevant compliance statutes. Review oversight red flags, actual board response, committee reporting and corporate law standards. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Draft an oversight-memo timeline, board committee charter amendment, regulator report framework and measurable remediation tracker. Include verified primary citations, adverse arguments, procedural assumptions, a two-outcome sensitivity analysis, and an evidence inventory for examiner review. The underlying lesson further requires: Prepare a chronology separating what the board knew from what individual employees knew and when escalation should have occurred under actual policies. Corporate oversight analysis is not identical to automatically imposing liability whenever a business has a violation. Identify reporting systems, board minutes, recurring warnings, management responses and control-testing records. Determine whether a proposed remediation agreement requires a monitor, independent validation or defined data reporting, using the actual instrument. Draft a credible corrective-action structure with board ownership, independent review, escalation thresholds and consequences for repeated failures. Consider attorney-client privilege and voluntary disclosure carefully without","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Committee claims it had inadequate reporting; shareholders argue directors disregarded persistent red flags. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Documented corrective responses versus prolonged deliberate disregard. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Corporate oversight duties under governing entity law; agency enforcement terms; relevant compliance statutes. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Draft an oversight-memo timeline, board committee charter amendment, regulator report framework and measurable remediation tracker. Include verified primary citations, adverse arguments, procedural assumptions, a two-outcome sensitivity analysis, and an evidence inventory for examiner review. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-5-11","threshold":"The core legal question in Real-case workshop — judicial review after Loper Bright is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Under Loper Bright courts independently decide statute meaning; valid delegated agency discretion can remain.","analysis":"The fictional record states: A federal agency adopts a regulation requiring operators to pay for a new monitoring process. The statute authorizes oversight but does not expressly describe this fee; the agency says its technical expertise warrants deference. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Loper Bright Enterprises v. Raimondo, 603 U.S. 369 (2024); Administrative Procedure Act, 5 U.S.C. § 706. Under Loper Bright courts independently decide statute meaning; valid delegated agency discretion can remain. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Brief Loper Bright; create a statutory text/delegation/record table; draft a 1,200-word judicial-review bench memorandum; identify standing, preservation and remedy; write two competing dispositions with citations. Variation: Congress expressly authorizes reasonable monitoring fees subject to a formula. The underlying lesson further requires: READ THE REAL OPINION: In Loper Bright Enterprises v. Raimondo, the Supreme Court overruled Chevron's instruction that courts defer to an agency interpretation merely because a statute is ambiguous. Under the APA, a reviewing court exercises independent judgment on relevant questions of law, although agency expertise may be persuasive. Do not infer that every prior regulation or every agency interpretation is automatically invalid; analyze statutory authorization, any delegation, the agency's interpretation, standards of review and procedural posture. BUILD THE RECORD: Identify the enabling statute, delegation terms, statutory limits, regulatory record, agency's explanations, submitted comments and plaintiffs' standing. Separate questions of law from","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Agency argues that technical context supports its reading and Congress conferred implementation discretion. Operators respond that policy expertise cannot create fee authority not provided by law. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Express monitoring-fee delegation versus statutory silence on compelled payments. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Loper Bright Enterprises v. Raimondo, 603 U.S. 369 (2024); Administrative Procedure Act, 5 U.S.C. § 706. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Brief Loper Bright; create a statutory text/delegation/record table; draft a 1,200-word judicial-review bench memorandum; identify standing, preservation and remedy; write two competing dispositions with citations. Variation: Congress expressly authorizes reasonable monitoring fees subject to a formula. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-5-12","threshold":"The core legal question in Regulatory investigation: subpoena response and defensible production is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Map statutory demand authority, responsive scope, privilege, custodian control and deleted mailboxes.","analysis":"The fictional record states: A federal agency serves a document request on a multinational distributor seeking six years of sales emails and consumer complaints. An internal investigation has already identified shared-mailbox deletions. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Administrative Procedure Act; applicable agency enabling statute; privilege and document-preservation law. Map statutory demand authority, responsive scope, privilege, custodian control and deleted mailboxes. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Submit a legal-authority and deadline ledger, a 15-custodian sample collection table, privilege-log schema, phased production proposal, draft meet-and-confer letter and 1,000-word memorandum on limitations and enforcement risk. The underlying lesson further requires: START with agency identity, enabling statute, subpoena or compulsory-process authority, service, return date and avenue to challenge breadth. Do not assume every letter has subpoena power or identical enforcement consequences. Develop custodians and systems map: sales mailboxes, chat retention, CRM, consumer call center, personal devices if legally relevant, and foreign data centers. Separate responsive information from attorney-client communications and work product; privilege does not attach to ordinary facts simply because counsel reviews them. Test proportionality and burden under the actual forum's standards rather than importing civil discovery automatically. Distinguish a preservation directive from a legal requirement to produce every employe","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Agency says complaints and sales communications are centrally relevant; respondent says six years of worldwide data is overbroad, privileged materials must be withheld and third-party data needs protection. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Valid targeted agency demand versus excessively broad worldwide six-year collection. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Administrative Procedure Act; applicable agency enabling statute; privilege and document-preservation law. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Submit a legal-authority and deadline ledger, a 15-custodian sample collection table, privilege-log schema, phased production proposal, draft meet-and-confer letter and 1,000-word memorandum on limitations and enforcement risk. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-5-13","threshold":"The core legal question in Agency enforcement hearing: admissibility, hearing rights and sanctions is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Use applicable agency hearing standards, not blanket Federal Evidence Rules, to test records and civil penalty.","analysis":"The fictional record states: A federal safety agency proposes a large civil penalty against a regional carrier after an inspection. Its lead investigator relied on anonymous complaints, an unverified spreadsheet, and a company email obtained before a formal hearing. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Administrative Procedure Act, agency organic statute and applicable hearing rules. Use applicable agency hearing standards, not blanket Federal Evidence Rules, to test records and civil penalty. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Produce an evidentiary matrix, 20 hearing questions, 1,100-word findings proposal, penalty proportionality chart and narrow appellate-review checklist. The underlying lesson further requires: Determine whether a formal adjudication statute is triggered and what procedures the specific agency's governing law requires. Do not import all Federal Rules of Evidence mechanically into administrative proceedings. Create an evidence and confrontation fairness analysis based on applicable procedure, reliability, official notice and opportunity to respond. Identify burdens of proof and standards for reviewing sanctions from governing statutes, not generic assumptions. Draft an examiner's neutral record summary showing what is corroborated, contested or missing. Consider constitutional procedural protections where appropriate. Evaluate proportional penalty factors without making up a mandatory schedule, and identify what findings an appeal or review ","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Agency asserts public safety necessity and business-record reliability; carrier challenges foundation, penalty proportionality and notice of charged conduct. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Authenticated corroborated evidence with rebuttal opportunity versus anonymous unverified allegations. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Administrative Procedure Act, agency organic statute and applicable hearing rules. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Produce an evidentiary matrix, 20 hearing questions, 1,100-word findings proposal, penalty proportionality chart and narrow appellate-review checklist. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-5-14","threshold":"The core legal question in Compliance program assessment: whistleblowers and document retention is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Preserve documents, avoid retaliation, check statute-specific whistleblower coverage and investigation independence.","analysis":"The fictional record states: An international logistics group receives an internal allegation of false safety certification. Senior managers want to interview employees immediately, pause a document hold, and remove a whistleblower from the project while investigating. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Relevant federal whistleblower statutes, agency guidance and litigation preservation duties. Preserve documents, avoid retaliation, check statute-specific whistleblower coverage and investigation independence. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Draft an investigation charter, interview sequence, legal-hold memorandum, retaliation-risk map, and an 1,000-word report framing competing factual findings and remedial steps. The underlying lesson further requires: Build a parallel plan for legal hold, evidence preservation, triage of immediate safety issues, employee protection and credible fact-finding. Verify whistleblower protection law and reporting channels relevant to the specific industry and jurisdiction; do not assume all complainants share the same federal remedies. Separate investigator neutrality and privilege analysis from ordinary HR interviews. Warn against retaliation or witness coaching, and define precise escalation criteria for boards and regulators. Evaluate how a lawful retention schedule interacts with a credible anticipation of litigation or investigation. Develop a written investigation charter, communication protocol and remediation plan without predetermining the allegation's truth.\n\n","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Management invokes confidentiality and business continuity; complainant seeks protection and transparency. Both demand clarity on scope, timing and review independence. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Preexisting documented personnel change versus reassignment punitive for reporting. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Relevant federal whistleblower statutes, agency guidance and litigation preservation duties. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Draft an investigation charter, interview sequence, legal-hold memorandum, retaliation-risk map, and an 1,000-word report framing competing factual findings and remedial steps. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-6-7","threshold":"The core legal question in Negotiation ethics, truthfulness and client decisions is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Apply adopted state truthfulness rule to knowingly false material representations while respecting lawful confidentiality.","analysis":"The fictional record states: In settlement talks, one attorney knows that a client witness's prior statement was materially inaccurate, while the client instructs counsel to say the evidence is undisputed. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Applicable state professional rules including truthfulness in statements to others; Rules 1.2, 1.4, 1.6 and 4.1 as educational references. Apply adopted state truthfulness rule to knowingly false material representations while respecting lawful confidentiality. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Draft a 1,100-word ethics memo, lawful negotiation script, client consultation outline and alternative response to a direct question about the statement. Include a verified-source table, factual uncertainties, a second legally plausible outcome and the limits of your chosen remedy. Submit this draft for qualified human review. The underlying lesson further requires: Client loyalty does not authorize a lawyer to make knowingly false material factual statements. Separate permissible negotiation positions and value estimates from assertions about presently known facts. Identify exactly what the attorney knows about the prior statement, whether the evidence concerns a material fact, and whether correction is legally required or permitted within the client's confidentiality obligations. Do not assume the lawyer may reveal all confidential information; evaluate applicable ethics exceptions and the selected jurisdiction's adopted rules. Explain to the client the risks of a false representation, available truthful framing, and the need to withdraw from misconduct when required. Determine who has settlement authority and","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Client seeks leverage and privacy; opposing counsel demands a representation that the witness account has never changed. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Disputed facts accurately characterized versus knowingly false assurance of undisputed evidence. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Applicable state professional rules including truthfulness in statements to others; Rules 1.2, 1.4, 1.6 and 4.1 as educational references. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Draft a 1,100-word ethics memo, lawful negotiation script, client consultation outline and alternative response to a direct question about the statement. Include a verified-source table, factual uncertainties, a second legally plausible outcome and the limits of your chosen remedy. Submit this draft for qualified human review. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-6-8","threshold":"The core legal question in Law practice client intake and conflicts screening is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Distinguish current/former client status, substantial relatedness, protected confidences and consent.","analysis":"The fictional record states: A firm receives an inquiry from a new corporate client seeking to sue a company that the firm advised three years ago. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Governing state ethics rules on prospective, current and former clients; confidentiality and screening. Distinguish current/former client status, substantial relatedness, protected confidences and consent. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Prepare an intake questionnaire, conflict grid, consentability analysis, nonengagement letter and preservation warning. Include verified primary citations, adverse arguments, procedural assumptions, a two-outcome sensitivity analysis, and an evidence inventory for examiner review. The underlying lesson further requires: Before reviewing confidential documents, identify potential adverse parties, firm clients, affiliates and matter relationships. Distinguish a prospective-client inquiry from a full attorney-client relationship and consider what duties attach to information obtained during intake. Former-client adversity may require a substantial-relationship inquiry and limits on use of protected information; not every prior engagement forever bars unrelated adverse work. Document screening, conflict clearance and any informed-consent process under the applicable jurisdiction. Do not assume a conflict is consentable simply because both parties would like the representation. If counsel cannot accept the matter, draft a respectful nonengagement notice that avoids discl","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: New client demands immediate acceptance; former client contends the new lawsuit uses confidential strategic information. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Use of prior confidential strategy versus wholly unrelated new action. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Governing state ethics rules on prospective, current and former clients; confidentiality and screening. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Prepare an intake questionnaire, conflict grid, consentability analysis, nonengagement letter and preservation warning. Include verified primary citations, adverse arguments, procedural assumptions, a two-outcome sensitivity analysis, and an evidence inventory for examiner review. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-6-9","threshold":"The core legal question in Client trust, adverse evidence and withdrawal is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Separate valid privilege/protective relief from impermissible concealment of responsive unprivileged evidence.","analysis":"The fictional record states: During discovery, a commercial client directs counsel to conceal a damaging email and threatens to fire the lawyer if the email is produced. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Applicable professional-conduct rules on candor, fairness to opposing parties, confidentiality and withdrawal; civil discovery duties. Separate valid privilege/protective relief from impermissible concealment of responsive unprivileged evidence. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Prepare a privilege/relevance chart, ethics decision tree, client advice memorandum, draft motion for protective relief and withdrawal contingency plan. Include verified primary citations, adverse arguments, procedural assumptions, a two-outcome sensitivity analysis, and an evidence inventory for examiner review. The underlying lesson further requires: Separate confidential client communications from the underlying email's discoverability. A lawyer's duty to protect confidentiality does not permit unlawful suppression or destruction of responsive evidence. Identify the discovery request, preservation duty, objections genuinely available, court orders and whether the client's instruction contemplates misconduct. Counsel should advise against concealment, explain litigation and ethical consequences and consider lawful protective orders or privilege claims if actually supported. Determine when withdrawal is permitted or required and what notice, court permission or transition steps are necessary. Withdrawal does not retroactively cure a false certification or allow disclosure of every client confidenc","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Client fears financial collapse if the email is seen; counsel must honor lawful confidentiality while refusing improper concealment. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Properly logged privileged message versus hidden responsive email. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Applicable professional-conduct rules on candor, fairness to opposing parties, confidentiality and withdrawal; civil discovery duties. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Prepare a privilege/relevance chart, ethics decision tree, client advice memorandum, draft motion for protective relief and withdrawal contingency plan. Include verified primary citations, adverse arguments, procedural assumptions, a two-outcome sensitivity analysis, and an evidence inventory for examiner review. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-6-11","threshold":"The core legal question in Professional responsibility lab — conflicts, confidentiality and settlement authority is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Analyze direct adversity to current client, former-client constraints, and legal significance of settlement nondisclosure.","analysis":"The fictional record states: A firm negotiating for a software company receives an approach from a new client seeking to sue that same company. Meanwhile a junior lawyer learns a proposed settlement omits a significant data-breach fact. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under ABA Model Rules of Professional Conduct 1.4, 1.6, 1.7, 1.9, 1.16 and 1.2(a); verify local adopted rules. Analyze direct adversity to current client, former-client constraints, and legal significance of settlement nondisclosure. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Draft a conflicts matrix, a 1,000-word ethics memorandum, client consultation script, proposed decision record and withdrawal contingency. Describe how conclusions change when the litigating client was formerly represented rather than currently represented. The underlying lesson further requires: STEP 1 — Confirm the actual jurisdiction's ethics rules: ABA Model Rules are teaching models, not automatically controlling. Identify existing client, former client and prospective client duties separately. STEP 2 — Document who is owed loyalty and confidentiality; evaluate direct adversity and material limitation under the applicable Rule 1.7 standard, and test whether a conflict is consentable with informed written consent. Confidential information does not become free to disclose because it might be helpful in negotiating another client's case. STEP 3 — Separate settlement authority from litigation strategy. Under Model Rule 1.2(a), the client decides whether to settle; Model Rule 1.4 requires adequate consultation and communication. STEP 4 — Anal","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Firm claims its teams are screened and work is unrelated; affected client says the direct adversity and material limitation undermine loyalty. A client requests nondisclosure of a fact whose legal significance is disputed. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Truly former unrelated client versus concurrently represented litigating client. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of ABA Model Rules of Professional Conduct 1.4, 1.6, 1.7, 1.9, 1.16 and 1.2(a); verify local adopted rules. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Draft a conflicts matrix, a 1,000-word ethics memorandum, client consultation script, proposed decision record and withdrawal contingency. Describe how conclusions change when the litigating client was formerly represented rather than currently represented. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-6-12","threshold":"The core legal question in Client counseling simulation: settlement, informed consent and litigation risk is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Clients control settlement; aggregate settlement requires applicable disclosures and consent safeguards.","analysis":"The fictional record states: A lawyer represents two injured employees negotiating a global settlement. One wants to accept $90,000 immediately while the other opposes any deal without a public apology. The defendant offers a single lump sum conditioned on both releases. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under ABA Model Rules 1.2, 1.4, 1.7, 1.8(g); state-adopted professional conduct rules control. Clients control settlement; aggregate settlement requires applicable disclosures and consent safeguards. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Create two client interview scripts, an informed-consent checklist, a written options letter to each client, a conflict escalation protocol, a 1,000-word ethics memorandum, and a sample consent document subject to jurisdiction-specific review. The underlying lesson further requires: Begin with engagement scope, current conflicts, whether aggregate settlement rules apply, and identity of each client decision-maker. The client—not the lawyer—ordinarily decides whether to settle. When two or more clients are involved in an aggregate settlement, evaluate the jurisdiction's informed-consent requirements and what disclosures each needs about other clients' participation and allocation. Explain to both clients the material risks, litigation costs, insurance and collectability uncertainties, confidentiality terms, releases and any joint representation limits. Do not assume a private conversation with one client can be freely disclosed to the other under the governing engagement terms. Provide tailored advice without pressuring either in","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: One client prefers certainty; the other prioritizes accountability. Lawyer says a global deal is objectively better but risks substituting counsel's preference for each client's judgment. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Separate individually authorized deals versus forced combined release contrary to one client's wishes. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of ABA Model Rules 1.2, 1.4, 1.7, 1.8(g); state-adopted professional conduct rules control. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Create two client interview scripts, an informed-consent checklist, a written options letter to each client, a conflict escalation protocol, a 1,000-word ethics memorandum, and a sample consent document subject to jurisdiction-specific review. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-6-13","threshold":"The core legal question in Confidentiality and privilege clinic: joint representation and internal investigations is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Entity counsel represents corporation; explain organizational warnings, privileges and individual conflict.","analysis":"The fictional record states: A corporation's lawyer investigates allegations by an officer. During interviews, employees assume the attorney represents them personally. Two executives later request separate privileged advice and ask for the interview transcript. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under ABA Model Rules 1.6, 1.7, 1.13 and state counterparts; governing privilege law. Entity counsel represents corporation; explain organizational warnings, privileges and individual conflict. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Write a warning script with limits, engagement-status chart, 1,000-word privilege memo, employee FAQ and protocol for transferring investigative materials to external counsel. The underlying lesson further requires: Identify the entity as the client in an organizational representation and assess whether any individual representation has begun. Explain the importance of an accurate organizational representation warning and limits of privilege rather than relying on a script alone. Examine joint representation conflicts, confidentiality of information, possible privilege waiver and rights to access notes under governing law. Distinguish privilege, ethical confidentiality and work-product protection, which can overlap but are not interchangeable. Formulate safe communications to employees, independent counsel referral criteria and a plan for conflicts that emerge during investigation. A lawyer should avoid guaranteeing privilege before actual legal conditions are e","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Employee argues they thought counsel represented them; corporation asserts entity privilege and a legitimate business need to examine conduct. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Clear Upjohn explanation versus individual legal assurances generating separate representation expectations. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of ABA Model Rules 1.6, 1.7, 1.13 and state counterparts; governing privilege law. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Write a warning script with limits, engagement-status chart, 1,000-word privilege memo, employee FAQ and protocol for transferring investigative materials to external counsel. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-6-14","threshold":"The core legal question in Mediation practicum: deal design and informed settlement authority is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Assess each release, restriction, confidentiality and asset-division clause under state mediation/contract law.","analysis":"The fictional record states: Two business partners litigate over a failed franchise expansion. A proposed settlement would divide remaining assets, release unknown claims, bar future competition for five years, and require each party to accept a confidentiality restriction. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Applicable state mediation confidentiality law; ABA Model Rules 1.2, 1.4 and 1.7. Assess each release, restriction, confidentiality and asset-division clause under state mediation/contract law. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Produce a six-issue negotiation plan, two alternative term sheets, a client decision sheet, a 900-word enforceability memo and a hypothetical mediation closing checklist. The underlying lesson further requires: Identify each client and decision-maker and verify counsel's authority to settle. Treat mediation statements and privilege as jurisdiction-specific, not universally protected. Examine collectability, tax and employment impacts where appropriate, enforceability of noncompetition restrictions, scope of releases, default remedies and third-party claims. Draft a term sheet defining assets, valuation mechanics, release carve-outs, dispute resolution and implementation timetable. Include a plain-language risks letter for each represented party. Distinguish the lawyer's recommendation from the client's actual choice. A negotiating gain is not a substitute for informed consent or accurate disclosure.\n\nWORK METHOD: (1) identify the client, forum and procedura","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Partner A seeks a quick exit; Partner B wants continuing use of the brand. Overbroad release and restrictive covenants may frustrate an otherwise practical settlement. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Lawful narrowly tailored covenant versus sweeping five-year noncompete. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Applicable state mediation confidentiality law; ABA Model Rules 1.2, 1.4 and 1.7. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Produce a six-issue negotiation plan, two alternative term sheets, a client decision sheet, a 900-word enforceability memo and a hypothetical mediation closing checklist. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-7-7","threshold":"The core legal question in Integrated capstone rehearsal: competing legal deadlines is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Make separate regulatory, discovery preservation, insurance and board deadlines tied to actual triggers.","analysis":"The fictional record states: A multinational corporation receives a short regulatory response deadline, a discovery preservation demand, an insurer's notice request and a request for urgent board advice on the same day. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Applicable regulator-specific procedural law; civil discovery and preservation standards; insurance contract notices; corporate governance law. Make separate regulatory, discovery preservation, insurance and board deadlines tied to actual triggers. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Prepare a 72-hour action plan, four-forum deadline matrix, counsel responsibility chart, document-preservation notice, and 1,500-word board briefing with contingency options. Include a verified-source table, factual uncertainties, a second legally plausible outcome and the limits of your chosen remedy. Submit this draft for qualified human review. The underlying lesson further requires: Build a legal triage model that distinguishes hard deadlines, recommended internal milestones and tasks that need specialist confirmation. An agency notice can impose a different response window from a litigation discovery request; an insurance policy may have its own notice terms, and a board cannot waive duties imposed by statute. Create a dependency graph linking custodians, responsible legal specialists, decision authority and potential conflicts. Prioritize preservation and immediate safety measures without assuming every client document must be disclosed to all requesters. Identify which records are privileged, factual or regulated personal data under the applicable forum. Provide alternatives when the company cannot simultaneously complete all","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Agency urges immediate submission; litigation counsel seeks broad preservation; insurer conditions potential coverage on prompt notice; board wants a single confident answer. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Formal subpoena versus voluntary agency inquiry; separate contract insurance notice. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Applicable regulator-specific procedural law; civil discovery and preservation standards; insurance contract notices; corporate governance law. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Prepare a 72-hour action plan, four-forum deadline matrix, counsel responsibility chart, document-preservation notice, and 1,500-word board briefing with contingency options. Include a verified-source table, factual uncertainties, a second legally plausible outcome and the limits of your chosen remedy. Submit this draft for qualified human review. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-7-8","threshold":"The core legal question in Multi-jurisdiction litigation coordination and settlement allocation is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Map each claimant's governing law, damages, insurer role and separate public enforcement before settlement.","analysis":"The fictional record states: Three customers sue a manufacturer in different states, and a regulator issues a related inquiry while the business negotiates a global resolution. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Federal and state procedure; applicable contract and product liability laws; settlement and contribution principles. Map each claimant's governing law, damages, insurer role and separate public enforcement before settlement. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Prepare a multi-forum issue chart, claimant-specific damages table, global settlement term sheet, consent matrix and board advice memo. Include verified primary citations, adverse arguments, procedural assumptions, a two-outcome sensitivity analysis, and an evidence inventory for examiner review. The underlying lesson further requires: Begin with a separate claim map for each customer, identifying parties, forum, contractual terms, alleged defect, limitations periods and remedial theories. A global strategy must not treat distinct state law as interchangeable. Determine whether any consolidation or coordination is procedurally available; multidistrict litigation can coordinate pretrial matters without guaranteeing a single trial or overriding substantive law. Assess how settlement allocation, insurance coverage and indemnity affect parties differently. Account for regulator obligations independently and avoid promising confidentiality that would defeat lawful disclosures. Build a damages reconciliation to prevent double recovery while preserving legitimate separate claims. Identify","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Manufacturer favors a global payment cap; one claimant argues its injuries and governing law require distinct treatment. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: One claimant's serious distinct injury versus undifferentiated global cap. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Federal and state procedure; applicable contract and product liability laws; settlement and contribution principles. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Prepare a multi-forum issue chart, claimant-specific damages table, global settlement term sheet, consent matrix and board advice memo. Include verified primary citations, adverse arguments, procedural assumptions, a two-outcome sensitivity analysis, and an evidence inventory for examiner review. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-7-9","threshold":"The core legal question in Capstone portfolio rehearsal and examiner response is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Verify suspect judicial citation, audit net-loss computation and fill missing regulator timeline before board reliance.","analysis":"The fictional record states: A simulated general counsel submits a board memorandum based on an unverified court citation, incomplete loss estimate and missing regulatory timeline. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Governing ethical and procedural standards; independent source verification and course-specific authority. Verify suspect judicial citation, audit net-loss computation and fill missing regulator timeline before board reliance. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Produce a revised 1,800-word general counsel memorandum, case-validation log, corrected financial scenario model, deadlines register and examiner response sheet. Include verified primary citations, adverse arguments, procedural assumptions, a two-outcome sensitivity analysis, and an evidence inventory for examiner review. The underlying lesson further requires: Treat the portfolio as a professional work product subject to falsifiable review rather than a general writing exercise. Identify each proposition relying on the doubtful case, obtain the actual primary opinion and check that its holding, court, date and jurisdiction fit the assertion; if verification fails, remove or qualify the proposition instead of inventing support. Rebuild financial analysis with inputs, ranges and missing documentation. Assemble a multi-forum deadline chronology from authentic process documents, including any uncertain trigger dates. Develop the best opposing theory and show how it affects the recommended remedy. Draft a revised board-facing summary that identifies what can be done now and which decisions require qualified spe","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: A reviewer challenges the underlying precedent and calculations; student must correct and defend the reasoning instead of doubling down. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Overruled precedent or gross-sales damages versus validated case and adjusted costs. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Governing ethical and procedural standards; independent source verification and course-specific authority. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Produce a revised 1,800-word general counsel memorandum, case-validation log, corrected financial scenario model, deadlines register and examiner response sheet. Include verified primary citations, adverse arguments, procedural assumptions, a two-outcome sensitivity analysis, and an evidence inventory for examiner review. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-7-11","threshold":"The core legal question in Integrated general counsel simulation — acquisition meets cyber incident is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Distinguish access from proven data acquisition, buyer contractual remedies, policy notice and statutory triggers.","analysis":"The fictional record states: Three days before a Delaware company's acquisition closes, its security team discovers unauthorized access to customer records. The buyer demands a price reduction, the insurer asks for a privileged report, and a regulator sends a document preservation request. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Delaware DGCL §§141, 251; contract terms; federal and state incident-notification law must be independently confirmed. Distinguish access from proven data acquisition, buyer contractual remedies, policy notice and statutory triggers. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Produce a detailed incident chronology, legal duty matrix with jurisdiction/authority/status, an evidence preservation map, a 1,500-word board briefing, buyer-seller negotiation term sheet, insurance notice draft and two outcome scenarios. Grade yourself against accuracy, uncertainty handling, adverse arguments, client communication and source verification. The underlying lesson further requires: Run a 72-hour fictional general counsel response across five workstreams: incident containment and forensics; evidence preservation and privilege boundaries; statutory or contractual notification analysis; transaction covenants, closing conditions and disclosure schedules; and coordinated board/insurer/regulator communications. For each workstream identify accountable owner, decision deadline, necessary evidence and the legal rule requiring jurisdiction-specific verification. Treat discovery of access as distinct from confirmed exfiltration. Determine whether a breach notice clock has begun only after the actual triggering statute or contract is verified. Analyze whether a material adverse effect clause, interim operating covenant or representations ","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Buyer demands a holdback; seller argues the issue is contained and not a contractual closing failure; regulator requires preservation; insurer reserves rights pending policy review. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Confirmed sensitive-record exfiltration versus unverified access. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Delaware DGCL §§141, 251; contract terms; federal and state incident-notification law must be independently confirmed. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Produce a detailed incident chronology, legal duty matrix with jurisdiction/authority/status, an evidence preservation map, a 1,500-word board briefing, buyer-seller negotiation term sheet, insurance notice draft and two outcome scenarios. Grade yourself against accuracy, uncertainty handling, adverse arguments, client communication and source verification. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-7-12","threshold":"The core legal question in Third-year capstone oral board examination and written counsel portfolio is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Sequence conflicts, legal hold, protected whistleblower investigation, subpoena and deal vote.","analysis":"The fictional record states: The student represents general counsel at an emergency board meeting after a whistleblower allegation, litigation hold, threatened regulatory subpoena and proposed strategic merger create simultaneous competing deadlines. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Integrated federal procedure, evidence, ethics and corporation law; controlling authorities depend on selected scenario. Sequence conflicts, legal hold, protected whistleblower investigation, subpoena and deal vote. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Submit a 2,500-word general counsel memorandum, 10-slide board-brief outline, 20-item authority ledger, litigation hold notice, risk register, alternative-transaction term sheet, 15-minute oral defense script and a reflective correction log. Suggested human rubric: authority accuracy 20%, factual application 25%, adverse arguments 15%, legal ethics 15%, remedies/practicality 15%, written and oral clarity 10%; minimum 80% and no critical integrity violation to recomm The underlying lesson further requires: This culminating exercise separates four skills that must not be reduced to multiple-choice: verifying law; extracting facts from an incomplete record; advising clients under uncertainty; and communicating disputed options and deadlines. First, identify actual and potential clients and conflicts. Second, draft an authority ledger distinguishing primary statutes or rules from secondary commentary and note all jurisdiction-dependent propositions. Third, triage actions that protect evidence and prevent irreversible decisions before broader investigation. Fourth, deliver competing advice: proceed, pause or restructure, stating legal and commercial consequences. Fifth, defend choices orally against skeptical questions from fictional directors. Sixth, comp","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Directors want immediate transaction certainty while counsel must preserve information, accurately assess privilege and evaluate unresolved regulatory facts. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Verified immediate safety threat versus unsupported allegations requiring controlled fact-finding. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Integrated federal procedure, evidence, ethics and corporation law; controlling authorities depend on selected scenario. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Submit a 2,500-word general counsel memorandum, 10-slide board-brief outline, 20-item authority ledger, litigation hold notice, risk register, alternative-transaction term sheet, 15-minute oral defense script and a reflective correction log. Suggested human rubric: authority accuracy 20%, factual application 25%, adverse arguments 15%, legal ethics 15%, remedies/practicality 15%, written and oral  Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-7-13","threshold":"The core legal question in Integrated performance task: family-law order and interstate evidence is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Use supplied fictional custody standards plus jurisdiction, admissibility of recording and procedural fairness.","analysis":"The fictional record states: A parent applies for a temporary custody modification after a teenager refuses an overnight visit and reports frightening behavior. One parent presents an undisclosed voice recording and a school attendance log; the other contests the allegations. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Provided fictional family-law statute and record; procedural due process and evidence principles; jurisdictional law must be supplied or researched. Use supplied fictional custody standards plus jurisdiction, admissibility of recording and procedural fairness. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Produce an 1,200-word bench memo; 10-item evidence table; two proposed interim orders contingent on findings; 12 client-counseling questions; and a 300-word change-of-facts analysis if a verified recording directly contradicts the intake note. The underlying lesson further requires: SIMULATED LIBRARY: For this problem only, assume Fictional State Family Code §100 permits temporary modification when credible evidence shows immediate danger to a child's safety; §101 requires notice and an opportunity to be heard unless a narrowly justified emergency exception applies; §102 directs the court to consider alternatives that reduce risk while preserving fair process. Treat those provisions as invented exam resources, not actual law. FACT FILE: Parent A seeks emergency relief; Parent B disputes the report; teenager's account is in a dated intake note, school attendance is verified, and the audio's origin is unknown. Identify forum jurisdiction, operative order, procedural posture, available witnesses, authenticity and admissibility ques","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Applicant emphasizes credible immediate risk and requests limited interim contact; respondent demands procedural fairness and challenges ambiguous recordings and unsupported conclusions. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Authenticated evidence of immediate danger versus disproved allegation and incomplete notice. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Provided fictional family-law statute and record; procedural due process and evidence principles; jurisdictional law must be supplied or researched. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Produce an 1,200-word bench memo; 10-item evidence table; two proposed interim orders contingent on findings; 12 client-counseling questions; and a 300-word change-of-facts analysis if a verified recording directly contradicts the intake note. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-7-14","threshold":"The core legal question in NextGen-style integrated bar rehearsal: contracts, negligence and business associations is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Analyze UCC tender, the director's actual/apparent authority, software causation and valid loss limitations.","analysis":"The fictional record states: An LLC orders 5,000 temperature sensors for $250,000. A supplier delivers 2,000 late and a software bug causes $40,000 damage to customer inventory. The LLC's operations director agreed by email, but managers dispute their signing authority. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under UCC Article 2 where applicable; contract formation/remedies; general negligence; entity authority and agency; fictional supplied terms. Analyze UCC tender, the director's actual/apparent authority, software causation and valid loss limitations. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Submit a 12-element issue map, 1,500-word counsel memorandum, 5 short answers (100–200 words), a sample settlement term sheet, 12 evidence requests, and a self-scoring checklist: issue recognition 20%, authorities 20%, factual analysis 30%, adverse arguments 15%, client advice 15%. A human assessor must evaluate substantive mastery. The underlying lesson further requires: MULTI-SOURCE CASE FILE: Purchase Order states delivery on April 15 and limits consequential damages except for willful misconduct, subject to enforceability; Sales Email says 'approved, start manufacture'; Test Log shows a previously known bug, but the signer says they never received it; Operating Agreement excerpt requires two-manager approval for purchases over $200,000; counterparties have historically accepted single-manager orders. PART A — Determine merchant and goods issues and whether UCC Article 2 applies. PART B — Discuss formation, conflicting terms and breach without assuming the purchase order alone is a fully integrated agreement. PART C — Analyze actual and apparent authority separately and note what information the supplier knew about","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: LLC says the director lacked actual authority and consequential loss is recoverable; supplier argues reasonable reliance on prior deals, disputed causation and contractual limitation. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Prior approved orders versus disclosed authorization cap; independent software issue versus proven supplier defect. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of UCC Article 2 where applicable; contract formation/remedies; general negligence; entity authority and agency; fictional supplied terms. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Submit a 12-element issue map, 1,500-word counsel memorandum, 5 short answers (100–200 words), a sample settlement term sheet, 12 evidence requests, and a self-scoring checklist: issue recognition 20%, authorities 20%, factual analysis 30%, adverse arguments 15%, client advice 15%. A human assessor must evaluate substantive mastery. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-7-15","threshold":"The core legal question in Integrated general counsel final: multi-front board crisis is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Separate securities filing truth, litigation preservation, buyer covenants, board conflicts and investigatory timing.","analysis":"The fictional record states: A public company faces a threatened class action, an internal allegations memo, a pending securities filing and a negotiated acquisition. Board members disagree on whether to disclose, preserve or postpone a vote. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Corporate law, federal civil procedure, professional responsibility and relevant regulatory law. Separate securities filing truth, litigation preservation, buyer covenants, board conflicts and investigatory timing. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Submit a 2,500-word general counsel memo, 15-item legal authority ledger, case chronology, draft preservation notice, contingency decision tree, 15-minute oral defense and a revised memo after altered facts. The underlying lesson further requires: Conduct a coordinated legal department response in stages. Stage 1: identify clients, conflicts, privilege, factual uncertainty, pending deadlines and preservation requirements. Stage 2: build an authority ledger separating confirmed rules from matters requiring local-law or effective-date verification. Stage 3: prioritize the first 24 hours, including counsel's factual investigation and protected board communications. Stage 4: compare securities disclosure, acquisition covenants, fiduciary oversight and civil discovery risk without conflating statutory triggers. Stage 5: prepare a board decision memo with options to proceed, pause or renegotiate. Stage 6: defend one recommendation orally against a deliberately skeptical board and revise after a new ","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Transaction team wants certainty and speed; litigation team urges caution; board demands a defensible process and records of dissent. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Confirmed inaccurate allegations versus continuing independent duty to preserve once triggered. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Corporate law, federal civil procedure, professional responsibility and relevant regulatory law. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Submit a 2,500-word general counsel memo, 15-item legal authority ledger, case chronology, draft preservation notice, contingency decision tree, 15-minute oral defense and a revised memo after altered facts. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."},{"id":"L4-7-16","threshold":"The core legal question in Third-year practical final: combined litigation, negotiation and appellate record is whether the client can establish the governing elements and the requested outcome from this record. Screen Rule 50(a)/(b), Rule 59, evidence preservation, appellate jurisdiction and offer expiration.","analysis":"The fictional record states: A fictional manufacturer loses at a jury trial after a business-contract dispute. The record contains a disputed email chain, a contested expert foundation and a written settlement offer that may have expired before the verdict. Begin with each required element or procedural prerequisite under Civil procedure, evidence, remedies, ethics and appellate practice. Screen Rule 50(a)/(b), Rule 59, evidence preservation, appellate jurisdiction and offer expiration. The primary teaching analysis should distinguish proved facts from allegations, identify the relevant document or testimony and avoid a dispositive inference where facts are missing. Apply this to the requested professional deliverable: Deliver a Rule 50/59 eligibility screen, 1,500-word postjudgment memo, appellate issue statement, mediation term sheet, budget/risk matrix and oral advocacy moot session. The underlying lesson further requires: Develop an integrated posttrial litigation strategy using the actual verdict form, motions record and preserved objections rather than treating every alleged error as appealable. Determine the procedural prerequisites for Rule 50 and Rule 59 relief, finality and notice of appeal timing. Analyze evidentiary foundation and preservation and separate trial counsel's tactical judgment from a viable appellate issue. Evaluate settlement authority, the client's commercial interests and possible postjudgment negotiated relief. Prepare a proof-of-prejudice chart under the governing review standards. When a new email suggests the opposing party withheld evidence, examine authenticity, materiality, procedural vehicles and preservation before alleging misconduct.","counter":"The opposing position in the case is: Client wants appeal and full damages reversal; opposing party offers discounted prompt payment; evidence may justify narrow posttrial relief or nothing. Test this contention against the burdens, record and actual authority rather than dismissing it categorically.","variations":"Outcome-changing variation: Preserved legal sufficiency objection versus unraised theory after adverse verdict. Prepare a revised fact/evidence table for each alternative, specify which legal element or discretionary factor changes, and explain whether relief narrows, expands or remains unavailable.","source":"Verify the full primary text and subsequent treatment of Civil procedure, evidence, remedies, ethics and appellate practice. Confirm forum, jurisdiction, amendments, effective date and whether any hypothetical rule in supplied materials is fictional; compare the audit status before relying on any conclusion.","exemplar":"A qualified provisional conclusion would first resolve the threshold identified above, then state what the available record establishes, what remains contested and which legal conditions prevent certainty. Recommended work product: Deliver a Rule 50/59 eligibility screen, 1,500-word postjudgment memo, appellate issue statement, mediation term sheet, budget/risk matrix and oral advocacy moot session. Explain why the opposing position might prevail and what new evidence would change this conditional recommendation. Do not claim a definite judicial result without completing source and factual verification.","status":"Draft case-specific educational worked analysis; independent attorney/faculty source validation and examiner calibration pending."}];for(const item of additions){const l=lessons.find(x=>x.id===item.id);if(!l||l.workedGuide)continue;const {id,...guide}=item;l.workedGuide=guide;}})();
